WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:04.840
Nowadays, it's easy to grow desensitized to
all the blood and guts of the horror movie

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:05.340
genre.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.940
But even the biggest cinematic thrillseekers
have to draw the line every now and again

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.820
on what they can stomach seeing on-screen.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:15.160
Here are some horror movies that had audiences
reaching for the barf bags — or racing for

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.000
the exit sign.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:17.960
The Hills Have Eyes

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:22.540
Alexandre Aja's remake of The Hills Have Eyes,
about a group of desert-dwelling mutants,

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:26.060
was unsettling enough just based on the characters'
physical deformities alone.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:30.260
So when a whole gang of the crazed cretins
started attacking one family in gratuitously

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:35.060
terrible ways, it was simply too much, sending
some theater patrons sprinting away.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:39.500
What made the film especially difficult to
endure was how no one was safe from the clutches

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.200
of these creatures — not even the dog or
the baby.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.920
Salò, or the 120 Days of Sodom

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:50.820
This 1975 Pier Paolo Pasolini pic still finds
its way on almost every best/worst list of

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.260
the genre, even decades after its release.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.579
It was an exceptionally graphic depiction
of the nightmarish scenario that might unfold

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:01.260
for a group of children who'd been abducted
by fascists in war-torn Europe.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.060
From mutilation to the forced consumption
of excrement, the most unimaginable horrors

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.560
were realized in this commonly walked-out-of
film.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.540
The film was inspired by some of the darkest
literary works of all time, including Dante's

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:18.310
Divine Comedy, and seems to explore the depths
of inhumanity presented in the existentialist

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:21.980
take on the nature of human evil presented
in the Marquis de Sade's Justine.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:26.340
Despite earning critical favor and timeless
relevance, it's still incredibly hard to sit

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:26.840
through.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.440
Trouble Every Day

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.700
Not everyone has a taste for cannibalism,
and the massive walkouts and festival booing

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.540
that the unrated 2001 horror-drama Trouble
Every Day by Claire Denis earned is proof.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.799
The movie introduces an American couple honeymooning
in Paris.

00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:44.350
But the husband is really on a mission to
track down the wife of a fellow doctor he'd

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:45.280
been obsessed with.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.479
What he finds when he locates the couple is
that the woman who's the object of his obsession

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:55.130
has a nasty habit of seducing men...and eating
them, living out her darkest desires in explicit

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:56.160
visual detail.

00:01:56.369 --> 00:02:00.640
The movie wasn't meant to be such a seat-emptier,
as Denis herself once said, "I don't set out

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:01.140
to shock.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:02.900
That's not my way of doing things.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:04.219
I don't think it's explicit.

00:02:04.219 --> 00:02:05.219
And the cannibalism?

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.869
It is how a kiss becomes a bite."

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.640
If you say so, lady.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.340
And while we're on that subject...

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:11.240
Bite

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.780
This gory flick is about the effect of a bug
bite gone very, very wrong.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.340
But it was the audience that really got infected
when Bite premiered at the Fantasia International

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:21.160
Film Festival.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.520
The pic's producers were reportedly prepared
in advance for the rampant upset stomachs

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.770
that would follow the screening and even passed
out barf bags to attendees — but that didn't

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:31.820
stop people from exiting the flick early.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:36.100
For those who haven't braved the vom-inducing
experience, it's about a bachelorette whose

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:40.680
celebratory trip to Costa Rica goes way off
course when she's bitten by a bug that she

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:41.840
thinks is harmless.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.560
But the infection turns her into a murderous
loon whose body slowly morphs into an insect

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:47.060
herself.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:52.080
Reviewers who panned the film chided its illogical
homage to David Cronenberg's The Fly, but

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:56.450
some still found favor in director Chad Archibald's
use of gruesome screen tactics.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.120
And almost everyone had to skip a meal or
two after viewing it, whether they liked the

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:00.820
movie or not.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:02.060
Caligula

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:07.099
This 1979 take on the Roman emperor, whose
notorious sexual appetite and penchant for

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:12.010
cruelty marked his tyrannical reign and prompted
his eventual assassination, was so exceedingly

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.420
violent that even famed reviewer Roger Ebert
took leave of it early.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.900
His scathing review was so harsh that he actually
feared it might entice audiences to check

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.550
it out simply because they, quote, "simply
cannot believe any film could be this vile."

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.989
He went on, writing, "Some of those people
were walking out of the [theater] before I

00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:30.400
did … others were sitting, depressed, in
the lobby."

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.920
Ebert's assessment was echoed by many of his
peers, as the film was consistently slammed

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.600
by even those who did manage to sit through
its full two-and-a-half hour runtime.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:40.160
Martyrs

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:45.680
Pascal Laugier's 2008 revenge horror flick
is widely regarded as one of the most unsparingly

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.060
violent movies ever made.

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:51.320
The film, which follows two young women on
a maniacal payback mission to hunt down their

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:56.010
former abusers, featured several scenes of
extreme depravity and sent many audience members

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:57.290
packing along the way.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:01.349
The movie was willfully provocative and prompted
revulsion even in viewers with a relatively

00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:02.460
strong constitution.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:03.420
Baskin

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.260
This unrated Turkish fantasy-horror movie
featured a group of police officers who inadvertently

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:12.260
waltzed through a door to hell…and left
many moviegoers heading right out the door

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:12.880
to the theater.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:17.579
The macabre movie was relatively well-received
by critics who savored all its devilish delights,

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.200
but during its debut, there were walkouts
galore.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:24.080
Baskin's writer-director Can Evernol admitted,
"the last few minutes were a bit much for

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:24.580
them."

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:28.520
Whether it was the nightmarish bloodbaths
or the philosophical overtures of the demonic

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:33.020
beings, there were plenty of disgusting moments
to be seen — and fled from — in this ghastly

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:33.520
movie.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.360
Probably not a great date movie.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:36.720
Probably.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.140
Thanks for watching!

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:40.530
